@node dokgen_stats
@section (dokgen stats)

@c %start of fragment

This module measures a tree: how much of it is code, how much of that
code branches, who wrote it, and which files cost the most to keep.

What it reports is a small set of numbers a reader can reproduce.  The
lines of code, of comment and the bytes come from the files themselves;
the decision points are the branching forms a reader can find with a
search; the authors and the churn come from the repository's own
history.  Nothing here guesses, and nothing here grades: a file with
many decision points is a file that branches often, not a file that is
badly written.

@example 
(analyze-source "(display x)\n")
 => ((blank . 0) (comment . 0) (code . 1) (decisions . 0))
@end example

The parsers are separate from the calls that feed them, so the suite
that checks a log can hand over a log written by hand instead of
building a repository.


@c %end of fragment

@c %start of fragment

